你在这里呆了多久了？我记不清了。我知道。九个月？一年？你在这里呆了多久了？我记不清了。我知道。九个月？一年？一年？这太疯狂了。

77
00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:14.000
I keep the lights on. See, it was all sensitive. It's not living. I mean, yeah, you're breathing. You're taking up the personal moral. It's bad. Start to see the f***ing thing there. Start to hear voices.
我一直开着灯。你看，一切都很敏感。这根本不算生活。我是说，对，你在呼吸。你在占用个人道德空间。这太糟了。开始看到他妈的那些东西在那儿。开始听到声音。

78
00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:52.000
Oh my God. Keep you here until they break. I feel like I'm gonna throw up. Karamit, I'm wondering how accurate that description is of what lives actually like in solitary confinement? I do think that the disembodied voice that you hear talking to a paper is accurate on a number of levels. The voice is kind of flattened and affect, and the prisoner was describing hearing voices, hallucinations. That's a very common side effect of isolation. And people talk about time, the way they perceive time-changing
天哪。留着你直到你崩溃。我觉得我想吐。卡拉梅特，我想知道这种对单人隔离中真实生活的描述有多准确？我确实认为，你听到对派珀说话的那个虚无飘渺的声音在多个层面上是准确的。那个声音有点扁平且缺乏情感，而囚犯描述的是听到声音、幻觉。这是隔离的一个非常普遍的副作用。人们谈论时间，他们感知时间流逝的方式发生了变化，

79
00:38:52.000 --> 00:39:25.000
because there is no way to mark time. People talk about it exactly as that prisoner said, it's not that it even feels long. It's just that it's almost endless. That days can kind of in a weird counterintuitive way fly by because there's nothing marking anything about a day or a week. So in that sense, it's accurate. I would have spent a moment talking about your own role in looking at this.
因为没有办法标记时间。人们的说法完全就像那个囚犯所说的那样，并不是说它甚至感觉很长。只是它几乎是无止境的。由于没有任何东西来标记一天或一周的任何事情，日子可以以一种奇怪的、违反直觉的方式飞逝。从这个意义上说，它是准确的。我想花点时间谈谈你自己在审视这个问题时所扮演的角色。

80
00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:55.000
On the one hand, you're a researcher who has spend time studying the question, but as you yourself have said, you're also a prison rights advocate. You've been an activist at Human Rights Watch and other organizations. How do you preserve your ability to be analytical about the subject, while you also have what are very clearly strong views about solitary confinement. You know, when I went to interview Carl Larson, he said, well, you're a Berkeley liberal. So he asked me to do some background reading
一方面，你是一位花时间研究这个问题的研究人员，但正如你自己所说，你也是一名监狱权利倡导者。你曾是人权观察组织和其他组织的积极分子。当你对单人隔离有着非常明确的强烈观点时，你如何保持对这个主题进行客观分析的能力？你知道，当我采访卡尔·拉尔森时，他说，好吧，你是个伯克利自由派。所以他要求我做一些背景阅读，

81
00:39:55.000 --> 00:40:32.000
and prove that I was serious and I would listen to him. And so I actually think in the process of doing this work, I have become more open-minded and been criticized by advocates for talking so directly and so extensively to prison officials. And so I've had the interesting experience of trying to keep the conversation open across a really broad spectrum of perspectives on this process. And I think in that way, I've been able to try to at least incorporate these different perspectives and tease apart the arguments and the positions people are coming from. And I do think that in reform, prison officials have to walk into these institutions and engage with
证明我是认真的，并且我会听他讲。因此我实际上认为，在做这项工作的过程中，我变得更加思想开放了，并且因为与监狱官员进行如此直接和广泛的对话而受到倡导者的批评。因此，我经历了一段有趣的经历，试图在这个过程的极其广泛的观点谱系中保持对话的开放。我认为通过这种方式，我至少能够试图将这些不同的 perspectivas（观点）结合起来，并剖析这些论点和人们的立场。而且我确实认为，在改革中，监狱官员必须走进这些机构，日复一日地与

82
00:40:32.000 --> 00:41:06.000
these prisoners day in and day out, and they also need to be part of the reform conversation. And through this process, you know, surprisingly, even though I came basically from the other side, if you want to think of it in terms of sides, I've come to see that perspective. Karamit, what sorts of things do people in solitary confinement say they miss? What are the kind of things that you miss that the rest of us might not think about? So people talk about not having seen the moon in years or decades and how much they miss that. And then people talk about missing just pure human
这些囚犯打交道，他们也需要成为改革对话的一部分。通过这个过程，你知道，令人惊讶的是，尽管我基本上是从对立面来的——如果你想从阵营的角度来考虑的话，我已经开始理解那种视角了。卡拉梅特，身处单人隔离中的人说他们最想念的是什么样的事情？有哪些是你怀念而我们其他人可能不会想到的事情？所以人们谈论的是已经好几年或几十年没有见过月亮了，以及他们多么怀念这一点。然后人们谈论的是仅仅怀念纯粹的人类

83
00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:45.000
touch. And you know, I tell a story in the book about a prisoner who, his cell door and the cell door of the prisoner next to him were accidentally opened at the same time and they were rival gang members. But they had been talking to each other, shouting through the, through the cell walls. And when the cell doors opened, they just reached around and grabbed each other's hands and held on, because it had been so long since either of them had had a gentle human touch like that. Prisoners often stay alone in their cells for 22 or 23 hours a day, and you found that perhaps the only way to actually manage this psychologically is to stick to
触觉。你知道，我在书里讲了一个故事，一个囚犯，他的牢房门和旁边囚犯的牢房门同时被意外打开了，他们是敌对帮派的成员。但他们一直在隔着牢房墙壁互相交谈、大喊大叫。当牢房门打开时，他们只是伸手过去抓住了对方的手并紧紧握住，因为他们两个人都已经有太长时间没有感受到那种温柔的人类触感了。囚犯们通常每天独自呆在牢房里22或23个小时，你发现心理上应对这一情况的唯一方法可能就是坚持

84
00:41:45.000 --> 00:42:18.000
a series of very, very rigid routines. Tell me about those routines. The prisoners who I was able to interview in this research to understand their experiences tended to be the prisoners who survived. And so they did develop all kinds of coping mechanisms. And one of the ones I heard about again and again was that they would wake up, you know, first thing in the morning, 5 a.m., and they would do thousands of repetitive exercises, often what prisoners call burpees, so a combination of jumping jacks, push-ups, and sit-ups.
一系列非常非常严格的常规。跟我讲讲这些常规。在这项研究中我有幸采访到以了解他们经历的囚犯，往往都是那些幸存下来的人。因此，他们确实开发了各种应对机制。我一次又一次听到的一种机制是，他们会在一大清早，比如早上5点醒来，然后做数千次重复性的运动，通常是囚犯们所说的“波比跳”（burpees），也就是开合跳、俯卧撑和仰卧瘫（应为仰卧起坐）的结合。

85
00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:41.000
And, you know, literally a few thousand in the morning to start out their day. And then clean their cells, write letters, work on legal cases. And in general in these units, if prisoners are following the rules and they have money being sent in from family, they can buy either a TV or a radio. And prisoners who develop these routines talk about really limiting the time
你知道，早上字面意义上要做几千个来开始他们的一天。然后打扫牢房，写信，处理法律案件。通常在这些单元里，如果囚犯遵守规则并且有家人汇款进来，他们可以买一台电视或收音机。那些建立起这些常规的囚犯谈到，他们真正限制了自己

86
00:42:41.000 --> 00:43:10.000
they spent listening to media. So maybe only an hour a day or two hours a day or a special show they like to watch so that they were keeping both their bodies and their minds really busy consciously over the course of a day. And interestingly, prisoners talk about having trouble letting go of these routines once they got out of prison, that they would still do those thousands of burpees every morning at 5am when they got up, and that their ability to control everything in their space in that 8x10 cell they live in is also hard to let go of. That they could keep, you know,
听媒体的时间——比如可能每天只有一两个小时，或者看一个他们喜欢的特别节目——这样他们就有意识地在一天当中让自己的身体和大脑都非常忙碌。有趣的是，囚犯们谈到，当他们出狱后，很难戒掉这些常规，当他们每天早上5点起床时，他们仍然会做那几千个波比跳，而且他们在他们生活的那个8x10英尺的牢房里控制自己空间内一切的能力也很难放下。他们可以保持，你知道的，

87
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:58.000
President described to me how he could keep the cap of his toothpaste perfectly clean, and it was really hard when he had a roommate when he got out of prison. The fact that he couldn't control the cap of the toothpaste anymore. So, it kind of gives you a sense of how intense it is to survive and then how long those coping mechanisms linger afterwards. You talk in the book, Caramette, about inmates trying a number of different things to not just be physically active and mentally active, but emotionally expressive to try and find ways to do artistic things. Can you talk about that for a moment? Prisoners do struggle to find ways to express themselves. Sometimes that's becoming really good at the law and litigating cases, but very often it's teaching themselves to draw,
一个囚犯向我描述他是如何把牙膏盖保持得一尘不染的，当他出狱后有了室友时，这真的很难改变。他再也无法控制牙膏盖了。所以，这在某种程度上让你体会到生存是多么激烈，以及那些应对机制在事后会持续多久。卡拉梅特，你在书中谈到，囚犯们尝试了许多不同的事情，不仅是为了保持身体和心理的活跃，还要表达情感，试图寻找进行艺术创作的方法。你能谈谈那个吗？囚犯们确实在努力寻找表达自我的方式。有时那是变得非常擅长法律和诉讼案件，但通常是自学画画，

88
00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:29.000
sometimes teaching themselves to speak a new language, sometimes teaching themselves to sew. One of my favorite stories was a prisoner who told me that he was in isolation for a number of months, and I'm not even sure how he made himself a needle. I know that he pulled threads out of the jumpsuits they're given in order to make thread, and then he started tailoring his clothes so that they fit better. They're often given these very loose jumpsuits, and so he would add cuffs or shorten the sleeves. And I think other prisoners found out about him doing this.
有时自学说一门新语言，有时自学缝纫。我最喜欢的故事之一是一个囚犯告诉我，他在隔离室里呆了好几个月，我甚至不确定他是怎么给自己做一个针的。我知道他是从发给他们的连体工服抽线来做线，然后他开始裁剪他的衣服以便更合身。发给他们的通常是这些非常宽松的连体衣，所以他会加上袖口或者缩短袖子。我想其他囚犯发现他做这事。

89
00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:48.000
And in some cases, a friendly officer would pass a uniform back and forth. And he started tailoring other people's uniforms. It's kind of not that anyone is even seeing them. But it's this amazing kind of self-expression and community that they're managing to create in this place. I'm sure they're going to be people who say, look, there are lots of people in these units
在某些情况下，一个友好的警官会把制服传来传去。他开始为其他人改制服。这甚至谈不上有人能看到它们。但这是他们在这种地方设法创造的一种了不起的自我表达和社群。我确信会有这样的人说，听着，这些单元里有很多人

90
00:44:48.000 --> 00:45:08.000
who really are the worst of the worst. Maybe not all of them are, but some of them probably are. Some of them probably are really violent and really dangerous, and ought to be there. And I'm wondering, do you ever feel that they might be people who need to be in solitary confinement? So there are certainly people in isolation who are dangerous.
确实是十恶不赦之徒。也许并非所有人都是，但其中一些人可能确实是。其中一些人可能真的很暴力、真的很危险，理应待在那里。我想知道，你是否曾觉得他们可能确实是需要被关进单人隔离室的人呢？所以，隔离室里确实关着一些危险分子。

91
00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:46.000
One of the really important points of analysis is the length of time people are spending in isolation, the fact that people, we're not talking about weeks or months, we're talking about years and decades often, and that even people who might have been fairly scary or dangerous in their 20s are unlikely to be that way into their 40s and 50s. And as we look at decades of these policies, we see that even some of the people who have been held up as the scariest, the system didn't control them very well while they were in it, and they're doing surprisingly well outside of isolation. So it really does call the practice into question on all different levels.
分析中非常重要的一点是人们在隔离室中度过的时间长度，事实上人们——我们讨论的不是几周或几个月，我们讨论的往往是几年和几十年，而且即使是那些在20多岁时可能相当可怕或危险的人，到了40多岁和50多岁时也不太可能是那样了。当我们回顾这些政策的几十年时，我们看到，即使是那些被认为最可怕的人，当他们在里面时，体制也没有很好地控制他们，而他们在隔离室外面时表现得出奇地好。因此，这确实在各个不同的层面上对这种做法提出了质疑。

92
00:45:46.000 --> 00:46:07.000
So your book comes at a time when many liberals and conservatives have joined hands to call for prison reform, for one thing, keeping people in prison and keeping someone in solitary confinement is very, very expensive. Give us a sense of how expensive it is and whether these ideological pairings are triggering any change in the system.
所以你的书出版之际，正值许多自由派和保守派携手呼吁监狱改革，一方面，把人关在监狱里以及把人关在单人隔离室里是非常非常昂贵的。请让我们大致了解一下这有多昂贵，以及这些意识形态的结合是否正在引发体制内的任何变化。

93
00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:38.000
So, solitary confinement is astronomically expensive. In states like California, it costs about $45,000 per prisoner per year to keep someone in the general prison population, and it costs about $90,000 per prisoner per year to keep someone in isolation. So the cost of running the facilities is really expensive because these prisoners in isolation, every need has to be met by someone working in the prison, whether it's delivering mail or delivering legal documents or getting them a meal.
所以，单人隔离的成本高得惊人。在像加州这样的州，将某人关在普通监狱人口中每年花费约45,000美元，而将某人关在隔离室中每年花费约90,000美元。因此，运营这些设施的成本确实很高，因为这些处于隔离状态的囚犯，每一个需求都必须由在监狱里工作的人来满足，无论是分发信件、分发法律文件还是给他们送饭。

94
00:46:38.000 --> 00:47:11.000
And so that's how the costs go up. And the cost of the facilities are also expensive to build these kinds of technologically advanced facilities and that's not even wrapped into that per-presenter per year cost. So I think that has been part of the reform conversation as you suggest that perhaps there might be a less expensive way to do this work. One other cost of isolation is that the vast majority of people even from long-term solitary confinement, ultimately get out of prison. It's 95 to 98 percent of all prisoners get out eventually, and that's surprisingly true
这就是成本上升的方式。而且建造这些技术先进的设施本身的建设成本也很昂贵，这甚至还没有被计入那个“每名囚犯每年”的成本中。所以我认为，正如你所暗示的那样，这已经是改革对话的一部分，也许有一种成本更低的办法来做这项工作。隔离的另一个成本是，绝大多数人——即使是那些来自长期单人隔离的人——最终都会出狱。95%到98%的囚犯最终都会出狱，令人惊讶的是，这也是事实

95
00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:45.000
of people in isolation too, and so there's the question of what are the social impacts of letting people out. And I think in combination, those economic costs and the social costs, people are beginning to think about alternatives, and that is a conversation that is cross- this political lines and prison as I suggested in many states legislators are initiating reforms some litigation has happened but in many states prison officials within the system are looking at the norms changing in the critiques of this practice and initiating their own reforms saying what can we do to reduce our reliance on this practice
对于隔离室里的人来说也是如此，因此这就产生了放人出狱会对社会产生什么影响的问题。我认为结合经济成本和社会成本，人们开始考虑替代方案，这是一场跨越政治阵线的对话，正如我在许多州的监狱中暗示的那样，立法者正在发起改革，一些诉讼已经发生，但在许多州，体制内的监狱官员正在审视规范的变化以及对这种做法的批评，并主动发起自己的改革，说我们能做些什么来减少对这种做法的依赖

96
00:47:45.000 --> 00:48:24.000
and initiating their own reforms, saying what can we do to reduce our reliance on this practice? Karamad Rider is a professor at the University of California at Irvine. She is the author of the book, 23-7, Pelican Bay Prison, and the rise of long-term solitary confinement. Karamad, thank you for joining me on Hidden Brain today. Thanks for having me. This week's show was produced by Maggie Pennman and Parts Shah. It was edited by Tara Boyle. Our team includes Reena Cohen, Jenny Schmidt and Renee Clarre. Our engineers are Andy Huthur and Jay Ciz. NPR's Vice President for Programming and Audience Development is Anya Grunman.
